  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
